  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Saturday...\n\nAs southwesterly flow builds in aloft, a pressure gradient over \nsouth central MT has been favorable to produce wind gusts for the \ngap wind areas of Livingston and Nye, producing gusts 35-55 mph. \nThe strongest of these winds have already occurred but there \nstill remains a 20% chance for gusts to 50 mph. Temperatures are \nexpected to remain mild in the 50s through Tuesday. Ahead of \ntroughing there is a signal for mountain wave activity along \nportions of the Bighorn foothills. 700s mb winds look to be on the\nlow end around 25-30 kts, but this will be something to watch as \nit approaches and hi res model data comes in. As troughing moves \nin from the west late Tuesday into Wednesday, ensembles have \ntrended towards more concentrated energy moving up from WY into \nsouth central and southeast MT. Current probabilities show around \na 30-50% chance for at least a quarter inch of precipitation for \nRosebud county west, with about a 60-80% chance for area \nmountains. With the trend of the upper level pattern, higher \nprobabilities may be in store for southeast MT. As far as snowfall\ngoes, area mountains can expect to see up to 5 inches. Light \naccumulations cannot be ruled out along the foothills Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday morning. As this troughing exits Wednesday \nand higher pressures move in, a tight surface pressure gradient \nalong the MT/Dakotas border is expected to bring breezy \nconditions. Currently, there is about a 40% chance for gusts to at\nleast 40 mph Wednesday. Ensembles then depict brief ridging \nmoving in late Wednesday into Thursday, keeping conditions dry. \nClusters and ensembles show a system to follow late Thursday into \nSaturday, but there are still multiple outcomes possible. There is\nmoderate to high confidence in cooler and unsettled conditions. \nTemperatures look to return to near or just below normal, in the \nupper 30s and 40s for the second half of the week. TS",
